```python
from math import gcd

def lens_classes(p):
    reps = set()
    for q in range(1, p):
        if gcd(q, p) == 1:
            inv = pow(q, -1, p)
            reps.add(min(q % p, -q % p, inv, -inv % p))
    return len(reps)
```

The set $\{q, -q, q^{-1}, -q^{-1}\}$ is exactly the class of $q$ (the relation is an equivalence relation, as the Lean file proves), so its minimum is a canonical representative. For prime $p$ the classes have size 4, except the class of $\pm 1$ and, when $-1$ is a square, the class of a square root of $-1$, which have size 2.
